so we've been tracking the rise of  coronavirus cases in the United States  and we started seeing the numbers spiked  on June 19th there was the idea in late  April early May that that was the peak  when we had 30,000 new cases a day in  April May we were hoping I guess I mean  I certainly was hoping that we were past  the peak at least the waveone peak and  it looked like maybe we were until July  June 19th when we started seeing cases  climb and climb and climb up to as many  as 60,000 new cases a day a few days ago  centered around California and a bunch  of red states including Arizona Florida  and Texas at the top of that list and  what we observed for the last 20 or so  days was that even though the cases were  spiking the deaths were staying pretty  low and you can see if you're looking  that at the chart of deaths  they've been trending down and all along  there were questions about how is this  possible what's going on now the most  obvious explanation maybe we would call  it the simplest explanation we could say  the explanation that requires the fewest  assumptions is that there's simply a lag  between cases and deaths  you don't die the day you're diagnosed  so when you see cases spike you would  expect that it would take some time  before those increased case numbers turn  into deaths there were other  explanations that we considered as well  is it possible that the virus has  mutated to be less deadly it's possible  we didn't have any evidence for it is it  possible that doctors are getting better  at treating coronavirus patients which  is decreasing the death rate certainly  possible could it be that more testing  means healthier people are getting  tested so even though you have more  cases the share of cases that are  serious could be declining and that's  why the deaths continue to trend down or  is it younger people getting coronavirus  who are less likely to get really sick  all of these are reasonable  possibilities to wonder about but if the  explanation is the first one which is  there's a lag between cases and deaths  we would expect that somewhere in the  range of 18 to 21 days after the cases  spike deaths would start to spike  18 to 21 days from the start of the case  spike is this week it's yesterday it's  today it's tomorrow and sadly tragically  yesterday Arizona and Texas both hit  single day death records dating all the  way back to the start of the pandemic  yesterday we had again nearly 60,000 new  cases of corona virus reported and  deaths in Arizona and Texas and by the  way also in California California is not  exempt from this but it's been mostly  the red Staters saying that it's Arizona  and Texas that weren't going to see the  death spike  they are spiking Arizona with a hundred  and seventeen deaths yesterday Texas  with eighty four deaths yesterday those  are sad these are not records we want to  be setting these are tragic new high  marks for coronavirus deaths in a single  day now I don't want to be alarmist and  I hope that I'm wrong and I believe that  I've been relatively sober in my  analysis of coronavirus from the start  the simplest explanation is that the  deaths are spiking because the cases  spiked 20 days ago and if that's the  case in another 10 days the death  numbers are going to look really really  bad and what I mean by that is cases  started to spike on June 19 so the small  spike in deaths were seeing now would be  related to the start of the spike in  cases on June 19th around July 1st the  new case numbers were dramatically  higher than on June 19th and if that  tracks as a leading indicator to  eventual deaths in another 10 or 15 days  we are going to have unconscionable  numbers coming out of some of these  states it's horrible when we're right  about terrible things and this is really  really terrible now if we want to find  something to look at that looks okay  there are still some states doing quite  well right now the states with the  lowest reproduction rate of the virus  are Connecticut Washington DC which is  not a state Maine New Hampshire  Massachusetts Rhode Island Maryland and  Utah  although the reproduction rate there  what's called the R  or the r0 it's it's starting to creep up  and what I believe is going on is  nothing that requires any kind of  particular insight the states that are  doing well can only hold off cases for  so long if other states are a disaster  because travel between states is  constitutionally is it's a  constitutional right in the United  States ingress and egress from in two  states and when you look at the county  level map of the United States and you  see you know the red and orange counties  the ones that are in trouble they're  right next to counties that are doing  far better and so I believe that sadly  unless there are some interstate travel  restrictions put in place which I'm  neither recommending nor do I believe  would necessarily be legal I merely  making a sort of mathematical statement  you're going to see some of the states  that are doing well start to do less  well if cases continue to skyrocket in  places like Florida Arizona California  Texas and many others I can't even give  you the full list now I I do think also  that there are some governor's including  our governor here I believe Charlie  Baker here in Massachusetts has widely  done a very good job and what I'm  worried about is they're now doing the  indoor dining in Massachusetts I got a  message from my gym that its opening  next Monday I think that this is a  mistake we've done so much work here for  the last four months to get down to only  having a couple hundred new cases a day  without a spike it's like every day  about 200 cases 200 cases it seems to me  given that it's summer given that people  can do outdoor dining given that people  can do outdoor workouts I understand the  business side of it it seems too risky  to me to do indoor dining or to open  gyms movie theaters and some of the  other things being discussed I won't be  partaking I've been doing the outdoor  dining it feels safe it's more than  enough it's warm out I think that that's  a risk for the states that are doing  well to do right now and I hope that I'm  wrong but the numbers are truly truly  scary
 

